## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, beginner-friendly plan for per-tenant log growth control without code changes; emphasizes safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Filesystem quotas
- logrotate
- tmpfiles.d cleanup
- Rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple per-container log dir monitor (mock)
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const threshold = 500*1024*1024;
const containers = ['ct1','ct2','ct3'];
containers.forEach(ct=>{
  const dir = `/var/log/${ct}`;
  try {
    const out = execSync(`du -sb ${dir}`).toString().trim();
    const size = parseInt(out.split('\t')[0],10);
    if(size>threshold) console.log(`${ct} over limit: ${size}`);
  } catch(e){}
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to verify no regression after changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests pragmatic SSH latency debugging in multi-tenant contexts.

## Key Concepts
- sshd_config flags: UseDNS, GSSAPIAuthentication
- DNS resolution path and NSS order
- SSH debug with -vvv and performance timing
- Safe, minimal config changes with a clear rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to measure SSH login latency (sanity-check)
const {exec} = require('child_process');
const start = Date.now();
exec('ssh -o BatchMode=yes user@host exit', (e, stdout, stder) => {
  const latency = Date.now() - start;
  console.log('latency_ms', latency);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- If latency persists after UseDNS=no, what’s next?
- How would you validate rollback and minimize user impact?

